Libras Glove — Sensor Acquisition Firmware for ESP32 (C++)

Inspect the existing project before modifying files. Identify the ESP32 model, existing source files, sensors, dependencies, and build environment. Preserve working code and use firmware/coleta/ if compatible with the existing project structure.

Hardware Pin Mapping

Flex sensors (analog inputs):

Thumb: GPIO34 (flex_polegar)
Index: GPIO35 (flex_indicador)
Middle: GPIO32 (flex_medio)
Ring: GPIO33 (flex_anelar)
Pinky: GPIO25 (flex_minimo)

MPU-6050 accelerometer (I²C):

SDA: GPIO21
SCL: GPIO22

Verify compatibility with the exact ESP32 board, ADC resolution, input voltage limits, sensor circuitry, and power requirements. Configure the specified pins explicitly and never assume unverified hardware specifications.

Requirements

Implement only sensor acquisition, calibration, validation, serial transmission, and diagnostics. Do not implement AI, gesture classification, word recognition, model training, or audio playback.

Read all five flex sensors and the MPU-6050 independently.
Transmit each finger's raw ADC value, calibrated value when available, and validity status.
Read the MPU-6050's X, Y, and Z accelerometer axes, documenting the units.
Support configurable sampling intervals and calibration parameters.
Detect invalid, saturated, constant, or out-of-range readings and I²C communication failures.
Continue collecting from functioning sensors when another sensor fails.
Use non-blocking timing and minimize sample loss.
Provide serial commands: START, PAUSE, STOP, STATUS, CONFIG, CALIBRATE, DIAG, and HELP.
Output consistent CSV records containing session ID, sample counter, timestamp, all five fingers' raw and calibrated values, accelerometer readings, and validity indicators.
Keep diagnostic messages separate from CSV records.
Measure and report actual sampling frequency and dropped samples when measurable.
Account for ESP32 memory, ADC capabilities, and serial bandwidth.
Deliverables

Provide only the necessary firmware, drivers, build configuration, dependencies, and documentation. Include the pin connection table, electrical precautions, installation and compilation instructions, calibration procedure, serial commands, CSV format, and hardware testing instructions.

Verification

Inspect the project first, implement the firmware, and compile it using the existing environment or an appropriate ESP32 toolchain. Verify file paths, CSV consistency, command handling, and sensor failure behavior.

Clearly distinguish code inspection, compilation, simulated tests, and physical hardware tests. Never invent calibration values, measurements, or test results.

